  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nNow that the clouds are clearing, we've get a spectacular fall \nweather day with high pressure that came out of the Canadian Rockies \nmoving overhead. However, all one needs to do is look at the h5 low \nover the northwest CONUS to see what will bring this pleasant \nweather to an end on Monday.\n\nTonight, We'll be under the influence of the surface high. Cloud \ncover will move into western MN, but skies will remain clear in \nwestern WI, which will setup a decent gradient in lows tonight, with \nclouds keeping temperatures up in the 50s in western MN, but the \nclear skies and light winds allowing for some places out around \nMora, Rice Lake, and Ladysmith to drop into the upper 30s. Given \ncurrent forecast lows with isolated upper 30s, there's no need for \nany frost headlines in the MPX area at this time. \n\nFor Monday, the h5 low currently out around Idaho will deepen while \nmoving to along the NoDak/Canada border. A lead shortwave ahead of \nthis low will take on a negative tilt, with h85 winds responding \naccordingly, getting up over 50 kts as the LLJ moves across the \nUpper MS Valley. PWATs will increase to around 1.8\", which is \npushing near the top of climatology for this point in September. \nWith that kind of moisture and the strong LLJ already noted, IVT \nvalues are near climatology max as well. This will support some \nefficient rain producers, but that lack of instability will really \nlimit the heavy rain threat on Monday. The main uncertainty for \ntomorrow deals with where the heaviest rain falls, most of the \nguidance shows a large band of 1-1.5\" inches of rain falling with \nthis LLJ forced rainfall, but placement of that band ranges from \nwest central through central MN and south central MN through western \nWI. With the uncertainty in placement of the heaviest rain, we stuck \nwith the NBM for now, which favors the northern solution. What we \ndid change from the NBM were the highs Monday. We took a good 5-10 \ndegrees off of NBM highs for Monday, with cloud cover and rain \nholding highs back in the 60s for most of the MPX area.\n\nGoing into Monday night, the LLJ and plume of high PWATS will \ncontinue shifting east, largely moving east of the MPX area. Even \nthough the surface cold front will be lagging to the west of the \ndeeper moisture plume, the fact that the LLJ and plume of high PWAT \nair moves east of the MPX area means that the next round of heavy \nrain and storms that develops Monday evening through Monday night \nwill be largely east of the MPX area, falling along and east of a \nline from roughly Omaha Nebraska to Wausau, WI.\n\nNext week, this front will run into an unseasonably strong blocking \nhigh over the southeast CONUS. This will cause the front to stall \nout from lower MO River Valley to between the Great Lakes and Ohio \nValley. We'll see multiple wave ripple across this front through the \nweek. The first one comes Tuesday night through Wednesday night. \nThis looks to largely impact northeast KS to southern Lake Michigan, \nthough the northern fringe of this rain shield does look to get up \ninto at least southern MN, which drives the PoPs Wednesday afternoon \nthrough Wednesday night that are highest across south central and \nsoutheast MN. The next wave arrives for Friday into Saturday and \nthis one looks to take a more northerly track, bringing better \nprecipitation chances to the MPX area. We're again seeing models \npumping out another 1+\" rain event with this system, so there's \npotential for some beneficial rains to both begin and end the work \nweek.\n\nNext weekend, confidence in the forecast, at least for precipitation \nchances, drops off. The intense southern ridge will finally start to \nbreak down as a slow moving long wave trough moves across the north \ncentral CONUS. The NBM PoPs are under 20%, but the Canadian and \nECMWF are starting to come around on the idea of a stronger wave \nembedded within this trough driving another chance of rain across \nthe region Sunday/Sunday night, so it's likely that we'll start \nseeing PoPs increase for the end of next weekend as the NBM starts \npicking up on this newer development in the models.",
